Found in a bug hunt, reproduced by the finder. I confirmed the code.

Foundation/Runner.pm:54-55, against the synthesized template at
Foundation.pm:758 (`$bin -p "$PROMPT" ...`):

    $command =~ s/\$\{(\w+)\}/$ENV{$1} \/\/ ''/ge;
    $command =~ s/\$(\w+)/$ENV{$1} \/\/ ''/ge;

PROMPT is ALREADY exported into the child's environment at Runner.pm:51, so
"$PROMPT" would be expanded safely by /bin/sh -- parameter expansion is not
rescanned for command substitution. Instead these two passes splice the raw
text into the command line before the shell parses it.

Two consequences:

1. Backticks and $(...) in a prompt become commands. A prompt is board
   content, and Markdown -- the normal way anyone writes one -- is full of
   backtick spans. Reproduced:

       prompt: "Run `id -un > .../EXECUTED` first, keep the budget under $500"
       START command=/bin/echo -p "Run `id -un > .../EXECUTED` first, ..."
       SHELL COMMAND SUBSTITUTION RAN: marker file contains: getty

   The agent also received "$500" as "00".

2. Every $word anywhere in the command is replaced, including inside single
   quotes, so the documented output-shaping technique breaks silently:

       configured: command: echo "alpha beta" | awk '{print $2}'
       by hand:    beta
       through foundation: awk '{print }'   ->  alpha beta

Cost: the agent silently receives a different instruction than the one on
the board, and backtick spans in board content execute as shell in the
board's own directory. Single quotes in a `command:` give no protection.

Fix direction: drop the Perl substitution entirely and let the shell expand
the variables that are already in the child's environment; if a template
needs values the shell cannot see, export those too rather than splicing.
Fixed in 80a922f by deleting the two substitution lines -- the variables were already exported, sh just had to be allowed to do its job. Proof both ways against pre-fix code via overlay @INC: backtick span in a prompt executed 'id' and handed its output to the agent as the instruction; after, one argument, glob unexpanded, nothing run. Inventory confirmed nothing was spliced that is not exported, so nothing had to be dropped. Foundation.pm needed NO change: _claude_command interpolates $bin/$perm/$turns in Perl at synthesis time, and the only shell-level variable is an already-double-quoted "$PROMPT" (proven by argc=6). START line now logs the template, which as a side effect stops copying env values -- a wrapper's API key included -- into a plaintext .karr.log.
